<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Parihaspora</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">8th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Parihasapura</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Parihaspora was a city founded in the 8th century by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lalitaditya-muktapida">Lalitaditya Muktapida</a>, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>, on a plateau near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srinagar">Srinagar</a>. It served as his capital and was filled with temples, monasteries and a great stupa. Later kings removed its materials, and only foundations and scattered ruins survive.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/parihaspora.jpg" alt="Parihaspora" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rayan Naqash · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ancient_Ruins_at_Parihaspora_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Parihaspora, also called Parihaspur and today Paraspore, stood on a plateau above the Jhelum River about 22 kilometres northwest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srinagar">Srinagar</a> in the Kashmir Valley. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> name Parihaspur may loosely be rendered as city of laughs: parihas means laughter and pur city. The Jhelum now meets the Sind Nallah to the northeast at the Shadpur confluence; in the past the rivers met closer to the city.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lalitaditya-muktapida">Lalitaditya Muktapida</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karkota-dynasty">Karkota dynasty</a> moved his capital here from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srinagar">Srinagar</a>. Kalhana describes the founding in the fourth book of his history: the king built his residence and four temples, among them one to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> as Muktakeshva, whose image he made with 84,000 tolas of gold, another with an image of Parihaskesana in as many palas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, and a copper Buddha that, in Kalhana's words, reached up to the sky. The main temple was larger than the one Lalitaditya built at Martand.</p><p>The city lost its rank after Lalitaditya's death, when his son moved the royal residence. Under Raja Avanti Varman (855–883 CE) Soya Pandit changed the course of the river, and the city suffered from losing its river access. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalhana">Kalhana</a>, Avanti Varman's son Shankar Varman took the good material of its temples and palaces for his new capital, Shankarpur (Pattan). During the war between King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harsha">Harsha</a> and Uccala (1089–1101 CE) Uccala took refuge here; Harsha set the place on fire and melted down its statues. Sultan Sikandar destroyed the temples completely in the fourteenth century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Only large boulders, some richly carved, and carved footings remain in place, and local people call the site Kani Shahar, City of Stones. Carved figures of seated and standing atlantes have been taken to the Srinagar Museum. M. A. Stein visited the site in 1892 and placed each building from its ruins; on a later visit he found that many stones had gone, used by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jhelum">Jhelum</a> cart road, and he persuaded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dogras">Dogra</a> king, with the British resident's help, to stop the damage.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Parihaspore” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He founded the city of <b>Parihaspora</b> and built the Martand Sun temple, whose ruins still dominate the valley.</blockquote><a href="/state/karkota-dynasty">Karkota dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
